drumee-infra (1.2.22) stable; urgency=medium

  * Do not declare the private BIND zone when own_certs_dir is set. infra.js
    nulls private_domain in that case, but on a LOCAL variable — data.private_domain
    stays populated, so named.conf.local declared a zone whose files infra.js
    then never wrote. named refuses its ENTIRE configuration over a missing zone
    file, so on a fresh tls_method=own host that is the difference between DNS
    and no DNS at all. Found by deploying 1.2.21 to a real host that had been
    switched away from tls_method=own: own_certs_dir persists in the rendered
    configuration even after the method changes, and only a zone file left
    behind by an earlier render kept named loading. The 1.2.21 template
    approximated infra.js's conditions; it now mirrors them.
  * Make the "bind9 is not installed" hint one that works. bind9 ships
    /etc/bind/named.conf.local as a conffile and setup-infra has already
    rendered one over it, so `apt-get install bind9` on an existing Drumee host
    stops at a conffile prompt — and with a non-interactive stdin dpkg fails
    with "end of file on stdin at conffile prompt", leaving bind9 unconfigured.
    The hint now passes --force-confnew, which costs nothing because the
    dpkg-reconfigure that follows renders our version back. scripts/baremetal.sh
    was already safe: it installs bind9 first, with --force-confold/confdef.
  * tests/native/dns-zone-config.sh now checks the invariant rather than one
    instance of it. Zone files are written by replicating infra.js's OWN
    decision logic, and every zone declared in named.conf.local must both name a
    file that render produced and load in a real named-checkconf -z. Two cases:
    a LAN self-signed host and a tls_method=own host. Verified to fail on the
    1.2.21 template, which declares two zones with no files behind them.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Sat, 01 Aug 2026 12:20:00 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.21) stable; urgency=medium

  * Deploy DNS automatically on the self-signed / private path. A LAN instance
    is the only thing on the network that knows its own domain: nothing
    delegates a private name, so without a resolver on the box it resolves for
    nobody — the zone for it was rendered in full under /var/lib/bind and then
    served by nothing at all. tls_method=self-signed (and any local_mode
    install) now stands up BIND9 for that zone, alongside acme-dns-server which
    already needed it for the DNS-01 challenge.
  * Decide DNS from the TLS method instead of inferring it. setup-infra keyed
    "install a nameserver" off ACME_ENV_FILE, a variable that is only ever about
    the certificate challenge — so tls_method=own got a nameserver it had no use
    for. postinst now exports DRUMEE_DNS_SERVER (1 for acme-dns-server and
    self-signed, 0 for acme-dns-api, caddy and own) and bin/install honours it;
    unset keeps the previous behaviour for out-of-tree callers. When DNS is not
    wanted and named is nevertheless present, postinst stands it down rather
    than let it answer for a domain served at the operator's provider — guarded
    by the setup-infra marker in named.conf.local, so a nameserver Drumee did
    not configure is never touched.
  * Recommends: bind9, bind9-utils. Not Depends: three of the five TLS methods
    have no use for a nameserver. scripts/baremetal.sh installs it in its own
    transaction ahead of Drumee, because a Recommends is not an ordering
    constraint and apt may otherwise configure drumee-infra first, leaving its
    postinst to start a nameserver dpkg has unpacked but not yet set up.
  * debian/config now db_sets tls_method=self-signed in local mode. It only set
    a shell variable before, so the postinst read the answer back as the
    template default and a LAN install arrived claiming to want ACME against a
    delegated public zone.
  * Fix the rendered BIND configuration, which named rejected outright. A
    single-NIC host has private_ip4 == public_ip4, so both halves of
    named.conf.local derived the same reverse zone and declared
    <rev>.in-addr.arpa twice — and named refuses its entire configuration over
    a duplicate, not just the zone at fault. The public reverse zone also named
    its file /var/lib/bind/<reverse_public_ip4> while infra.js writes it to
    /var/lib/bind/<public_ip4>, and both reverse zone files set $ORIGIN to the
    bare octets without the in-addr.arpa suffix, putting every record in them
    out of zone. Zone declarations now mirror exactly the conditions infra.js
    uses to decide which files it writes.
  * init-named no longer dies on its first command. It ran under set -e and
    began with `service named stop`, which exits non-zero when the unit does not
    exist — the normal state where bind9 was never installed. It aborted before
    generating the TSIG key that named.conf.local includes unconditionally, and
    bin/install runs it under set +e, so the whole failure was silent. It now
    reports a missing bind9 with the command to fix it, validates with
    named-checkconf before starting rather than after, and reports zone-load
    complaints without trading them for no DNS at all.
  * tests/native/dns-zone-config.sh renders the real templates and hands them to
    a real named-checkconf in a disposable container. Verified: it fails on the
    duplicate zone as it stood before this change, and all three zones load
    after it.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Sat, 01 Aug 2026 10:55:00 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.20) stable; urgency=medium

  * Remove the rendered configuration on purge. setup-infra renders its files
    rather than shipping them, so dpkg never tracked them and a purge left every
    one behind — nginx kept loading the vhosts of a package that no longer existed,
    and a later install with different settings inherited them. That is how a stale
    02-private.conf, still including an ssl/private.conf the new render
    deliberately no longer produced, stopped nginx dead: one leftover file, every
    site on the host down. postrm now removes exactly the files infra.js and
    jitsi.js write into /etc/nginx, plus /etc/cron.d/drumee and the whole of
    /etc/drumee, then reloads nginx. Named rather than globbed, so an operator's
    own vhost is untouched. Files belonging to other packages that setup-infra
    overwrites in place — postfix, MariaDB, BIND, mailname, Prosody, jitsi — are
    deliberately left, since deleting them would strip those packages of
    configuration they ship themselves.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 23:24:45 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.19) stable; urgency=medium

  * Serve the static assets. static_dir was derived from the runtime directory,
    giving /srv/drumee/runtime/static, while the drumee-static package installs to
    /srv/drumee/static. The nginx aliases for /-/static/, /-/images/ and /-/fonts/,
    the jitsi meet root, DRUMEE_STATIC_DIR, STATIC_DIR and the pm2 ecosystem all
    inherited the wrong path, so every stylesheet, font and image returned 404 while
    the page itself was served correctly — status checks on the page and the REST
    endpoint both passed and the UI rendered unstyled.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 22:22:12 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.18) stable; urgency=medium

  * URGENT: fix a hang that made 1.2.17 uninstallable. The check added in 1.2.17
    for whether the domain question had been answered ran AFTER db_stop had closed
    the debconf session, and a debconf command on a closed connection blocks
    forever reading a reply that never comes. Every configure of 1.2.17 wedged
    there — fresh install or upgrade — leaving the package half-configured, dpkg
    interrupted, and a postinst asleep on a pipe with no child process to explain
    why. The check now runs inside the session, before db_stop, and exports its
    answer for the configure arm to test.
  * Do not let an unknown debconf question be read as the previous answer:
    `db_get X || true` leaves the prior question's value in RET rather than
    clearing it, so a template that failed to register was read as whatever was
    queried just before it.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 20:20:56 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.17) stable; urgency=medium

  * Ask before re-rendering an existing instance, and re-render when the answer is
    yes. infra.js refuses to re-render a configured host, so every upgrade until now
    installed new templates and applied none of them — this is the first release
    that can deliver a configuration fix to a host that is already installed. The
    question appears only when there is something to lose (a drumee.json naming a
    domain, the marker infra.js itself tests) and defaults to no, so fresh installs
    never see it and an unattended upgrade leaves a running configuration alone. It
    states both consequences: declining means shipped fixes to the vhosts, routes,
    certificate paths, DNS zone and mail configuration never reach this host;
    accepting rewrites every rendered file from the stored answers, losing hand
    edits to them.
  * Reuse the secrets a host already has instead of minting new ones. This is what
    made re-rendering unsafe, and had to land first: a second render used to
    overwrite credential/db.json with a password MariaDB had never been told,
    locking the application out of its own database. The mail password matters as
    much, being embedded in the rendered postfix configuration and stored in the
    mailserver tables at once.
  * Refuse to configure on defaults. Only an EMPTY domain was rejected, but
    debconf's default is example.com — not empty — so an install with no preseed
    completed "successfully" configured for a domain the operator does not own,
    with BIND serving its zone and every vhost named after it. Tests debconf's
    `seen` flag, which is set whether a human or debconf-set-selections answered
    and persists across upgrades.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 19:36:16 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.16) stable; urgency=medium

  * Never render a TURN upstream with no host. The address was interpolated
    straight into `server ADDRESS:5349`, but it is optional — debconf leaves it
    empty unless asked — while the file was written unconditionally. An instance
    that had not been told its own address got `server :5349;`, and because
    modules-enabled is included at nginx's main context, nginx refused its ENTIRE
    configuration: every site on the host down, with the only symptom an upstream
    nobody had configured. All three variants are now guarded on the address and
    the conferencing domain, and render as comments only when either is missing —
    inert by choice, since a half-written stream block here is a host-wide outage.
    Checked by feeding each render to nginx -t rather than by inspection.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 18:40:57 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.15) stable; urgency=medium

  * Remove the private vhost when the private branch is skipped. These files are
    rendered rather than shipped, so dpkg cannot clean them: purging the package
    leaves them, and a reinstall with different settings inherits them. nginx
    refuses its whole configuration over one bad include, so a single leftover
    takes down the entire site. Switching an instance to its own certificates
    nulls the private domain, ssl/private.conf is then correctly not produced, and
    the 02-private.conf from the previous install kept including it — nginx would
    not start until that one file was deleted by hand. Sweeps exactly the four
    private-only entries the branch renders. The public side is deliberately left
    alone: acting on a falsy public domain would delete the vhost serving the
    instance.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 18:29:09 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.14) stable; urgency=medium

  * Export the certificate-directory variable that is actually read. postinst set
    OWN_SSL, which nothing reads; bin/install, infra.js and the rendered
    drumee.sh all read OWN_CERTS_DIR. So tls_method=own and tls_method=caddy did
    none of what they promise: ACME ran anyway and failed invisibly under set +e,
    the certificate directory stayed at the default instead of the operator's
    path, so nginx looked for certificates where none had been placed, and a
    private vhost was rendered for an instance that had supplied its own certs.
  * Warn when the certificate directory does not exist, since it is only honoured
    when it already does, and otherwise a certificate is requested instead.
  * State the layout nginx reads in the debconf question: one NAME_ecc
    subdirectory per certified name holding fullchain.cer, ca.cer and NAME.key.
    A flat directory of .pem files is never found.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 17:57:41 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.13) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix nginx refusing its whole configuration on any instance that has both a
    public and a private domain. Every vhost glob-includes
    infrastructure/routes/*.conf, and that directory held public.conf and
    private.conf, which were not additive route modules but two renderings of the
    same route set for two different server blocks. Both declared "location /-/",
    so one server block got it twice and nginx failed to start, leaving nothing on
    443. Collapsed into a single shared routes/app.conf; the glob is kept so
    jitsi and vendors can add their own files, which own distinct prefixes.
  * Access-Control-Allow-Origin is now a valid origin. It was a bare hostname,
    which is not a serialised origin, so browsers ignored the header entirely; it
    is derived from the request instead, which also gives the vendors and jit
    vhosts their own origin rather than the apex domain.
  * Remove route files left by an earlier release before writing, so an upgrade
    cannot end up with the new file beside the stale pair and fail as before.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 15:46:31 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.12) stable; urgency=medium

  * Ask how DNS and TLS should be handled (drumee-infra/tls_method), replacing
    the own_ssl boolean, which is kept so existing preseeds keep working. The
    default, acme-dns-server, installs BIND9 here as the authoritative
    nameserver for the domain; acme-dns-api and caddy validate over a provider
    API and need no inbound port at all.
  * Serve and certify vendors.DOMAIN and *.vendors.DOMAIN alongside jit.
    Each subdomain needs its own wildcard, since a wildcard matches exactly one
    label — own_ssl_path now says so.
  * Derive and export JITSI_DOMAIN, which was always empty. Once vendors made
    the value load-bearing, an empty one aborted the install in
    create-local-certs.
  * Optional WireGuard peer coordination, disabled by default: reach this
    instance from outside without opening a router port.

